WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.713
Back to the restaurant with four waiters and a board of order slips.

2
00:00:06.713 --> 00:00:13.146
When a slip has to wait, its waiter clips it to the board by the kitchen door, and picks up one that's ready.

3
00:00:13.146 --> 00:00:15.093
Four waiters, two hundred tables.

4
00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:19.277
In Java words, a slip is a virtual thread, a cheap Java thread.

5
00:00:19.277 --> 00:00:23.484
A waiter is a carrier, one of the few real threads that run them.

6
00:00:23.484 --> 00:00:30.409
Clipping a slip to the board is unmounting: its saved stack goes to the heap, where Java keeps its objects.

7
00:00:30.533 --> 00:00:33.412
Here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

8
00:00:33.412 --> 00:00:38.355
If waiting slips always go on the board, can four waiters ever freeze the room?

9
00:00:41.733 --> 00:00:44.369
Let's find out with a cache from our shop.

10
00:00:44.369 --> 00:00:52.088
The checkout shows each price in the customer's own money, so it needs exchange rates, like how many dollars one euro buys.

11
00:00:52.088 --> 00:00:58.991
A rates service on another computer answers in about 100 milliseconds, so the shop keeps each rate it fetches.

12
00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:02.941
Many virtual threads ask at once, so the cache needs a lock.

13
00:01:02.941 --> 00:01:05.246
Most of us would write synchronized.

14
00:01:05.246 --> 00:01:11.456
It takes the lock every Java object has built in, called a monitor: one thread at a time gets in.

15
00:01:11.456 --> 00:01:14.849
The code is correct, and the lab's first test passes.

16
00:01:15.567 --> 00:01:17.369
Now give one dish a hot pan.

17
00:01:17.369 --> 00:01:23.486
A waiter holding a hot pan can't clip his slip to the board, because he can't put the pan down.

18
00:01:23.486 --> 00:01:26.641
So he stands at the stove until the dish is done.

19
00:01:26.641 --> 00:01:29.860
Nobody has made a board you can hang a hot pan on.

20
00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:30.891
Not yet, anyway.

21
00:01:30.891 --> 00:01:32.887
So the slip stays in his hands.

22
00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:35.310
Now let all four waiters hold pans.

23
00:01:35.310 --> 00:01:40.127
Every slip on the board waits, even one that only needs a glass of water.

24
00:01:40.127 --> 00:01:41.447
The room goes still.

25
00:01:43.067 --> 00:01:44.425
Here's the mapping.

26
00:01:44.425 --> 00:01:46.068
The pan is the monitor.

27
00:01:46.068 --> 00:01:53.501
It's in the waiter's hands because on JDK 21 to 23, the JVM recorded the carrier as the monitor's owner.

28
00:01:53.501 --> 00:01:57.432
The slip stays off the board: the thread can't unmount.

29
00:01:57.432 --> 00:02:01.148
And the waiter stands still: the carrier is blocked.

30
00:02:01.267 --> 00:02:02.308
Why the carrier?

31
00:02:02.308 --> 00:02:07.907
If the slip left, the pan would seem to belong to the next slip that waiter picked up.

32
00:02:07.907 --> 00:02:08.753
So both stay.

33
00:02:08.867 --> 00:02:15.755
That's called pinning: a virtual thread reaches a blocking call, but can't let go of its carrier.

34
00:02:15.755 --> 00:02:20.016
On JDK 21 to 23, even a slip waiting for the pan was pinned.

35
00:02:20.733 --> 00:02:22.701
Let's shrink it to one waiter.

36
00:02:22.701 --> 00:02:25.653
The lesson's PinningDemo runs on one carrier.

37
00:02:25.653 --> 00:02:30.441
A small virtual thread, the ticker, notes the time every 50 milliseconds.

38
00:02:30.441 --> 00:02:36.739
Three others each take the lock and sleep 100 milliseconds inside it, in place of the slow call.

39
00:02:37.467 --> 00:02:45.051
If nothing pins, the ticker keeps its rhythm, and the longest gap between ticks stays near 50 milliseconds.

40
00:02:45.051 --> 00:02:46.611
What will JDK 21 show?

41
00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:53.037
343 milliseconds, about three sleeps in a row.

42
00:02:53.037 --> 00:03:00.784
Each sleeper held the only carrier for its whole 100 milliseconds, and the JVM counted three pins.

43
00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:01.371
Well...

44
00:03:01.371 --> 00:03:03.659
That's a long silence for a clock.

45
00:03:03.659 --> 00:03:06.552
And the ticker never even touched the lock.

46
00:03:06.667 --> 00:03:12.299
Now swap the monitor for a ReentrantLock, a lock class from java.util.concurrent.

47
00:03:12.299 --> 00:03:20.435
It records the virtual thread as its owner, and it waits by parking: setting the thread aside until someone wakes it.

48
00:03:20.435 --> 00:03:21.965
So it always unmounts.

49
00:03:21.965 --> 00:03:23.077
It's a cool pan.

50
00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:25.740
Same three sleepers, same one carrier.

51
00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:27.544
What's the longest gap now?

52
00:03:30.333 --> 00:03:35.424
102 milliseconds on this busy machine, and 51 on the next run, with no pins.

53
00:03:35.424 --> 00:03:41.653
The sleepers still took turns with the lock, but on the board, so the ticker kept its waiter.

54
00:03:42.367 --> 00:03:44.209
And slow is the mild case.

55
00:03:44.209 --> 00:03:49.878
In 2024, engineers at Netflix described Java 21 services that stopped answering.

56
00:03:49.878 --> 00:03:55.335
Every carrier was pinned by a thread waiting to enter one synchronized block.

57
00:03:55.335 --> 00:04:01.145
The thread next in line for that lock was unmounted, and never got a free carrier.

58
00:04:01.267 --> 00:04:09.641
In the restaurant, four waiters wait at the stove for the pan, while the slip that must take it next hangs on the board, waiting for a waiter.

59
00:04:09.641 --> 00:04:12.472
Everyone waits, very politely, and nobody cooks.

60
00:04:12.472 --> 00:04:16.305
The carriers wait for the lock, and the lock waits for a carrier.

61
00:04:17.033 --> 00:04:19.176
Then JDK 24 made that hook.

62
00:04:19.176 --> 00:04:26.953
Its design document, JEP 491, changed monitors so the JVM records the virtual thread as the owner.

63
00:04:26.953 --> 00:04:31.874
So synchronized and Object.wait unmount, like a ReentrantLock.

64
00:04:31.874 --> 00:04:34.255
The pan travels with its slip.

65
00:04:34.967 --> 00:04:41.086
JDK 25, the first long-term-support release with the fix, gets updates for years.

66
00:04:41.086 --> 00:04:47.055
There, the same demo with synchronized shows a 52 millisecond gap, and no pins.

67
00:04:47.767 --> 00:04:49.078
Which lock, then?

68
00:04:49.078 --> 00:04:50.852
It depends on your JDK.

69
00:04:50.852 --> 00:04:58.333
On JDK 21 to 23, blocking while holding a monitor pins, so put a ReentrantLock around slow calls.

70
00:04:58.333 --> 00:05:02.112
On JDK 25, leave working synchronized code alone.

71
00:05:02.112 --> 00:05:07.896
A few pans stay hot even there, and the next part of the lesson shows them.

72
00:05:08.600 --> 00:05:10.888
Here's the fix in Java and Kotlin.

73
00:05:10.888 --> 00:05:14.791
Java locks on the line before try, and unlocks in finally.

74
00:05:14.791 --> 00:05:17.348
Kotlin's withLock writes that for you.

75
00:05:17.348 --> 00:05:19.165
Each rate still loads once.

76
00:05:19.867 --> 00:05:22.877
So, could four waiters freeze the room?

77
00:05:22.877 --> 00:05:30.211
On JDK 21 to 23, yes: a slip holding a monitor can't go on the board, so its carrier waits too.

78
00:05:30.211 --> 00:05:36.079
A ReentrantLock, or JDK 24 and later, lets it go on the board, lock and all.

79
00:05:36.200 --> 00:05:37.101
Quick check.

80
00:05:37.101 --> 00:05:38.603
JDK 21, one carrier.

81
00:05:38.603 --> 00:05:43.934
A virtual thread sleeps 100 milliseconds while holding a ReentrantLock.

82
00:05:43.934 --> 00:05:46.787
Can the ticker tick during that sleep?

83
00:05:49.767 --> 00:05:50.031
Yes.

84
00:05:50.031 --> 00:05:56.905
The lock doesn't pin, so the sleeper unmounts, lock and all, and the only waiter is free for the ticker.
